## Prerequisites

Before running this, make sure you have:

* Docker Desktop installed (Windows)
* Python (only needed if you want to test scripts outside Docker)

## Docker Setup & Usage

## Step 1: Clone the repo

git clone https://github.com/your-username/stressflow.git
cd stressflow

## Step 2: Build the image

docker build -t stressflow .

## Step 3: Run the container

docker run --name stress-container stressflow

## Step 4: (Recommended) Run with limits

This is important — without limits, your system can slow down or hang.

docker run --name stress-container ^
--cpus="2.0" ^
--memory="4g" ^
stressflow

## Execution Flow

Here’s how it runs:

| Time   | Action     |
| ------ | ---------- |
| 0 min  | Start cpu1 |
| 1 min  | Start cpu2 |
| 2 min  | Start cpu3 |
| 3 min  | Start ram1 |
| 4 min  | Start ram2 |
| 5 min  | Start ram3 |
| 6 min  | Stop cpu1  |
| 7 min  | Stop cpu2  |
| 8 min  | Stop cpu3  |
| 9 min  | Stop ram1  |
| 10 min | Stop ram2  |
| 11 min | Stop ram3  |

So instead of everything running at once, load builds up step-by-step.

##  Logging

Every action is logged with a timestamp.

## View logs inside container

docker exec -it stress-container cat execution.log

## Copy logs to your system

docker cp stress-container:/app/execution.log .

## How It Works

* Uses Python `subprocess` to start each script
* Waits between steps using `time.sleep()`
* Tracks each process and shuts it down in order
* Writes everything to a log file for visibility

